top/consensus-assurance/src/consensus_assurance/adapters/runners/experiment.py:116:    \"\"\"Extract complete CA_EVENT lines, preserving runner-provided stream ownership.\"\"\"\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src/consensus_assurance/adapters/runners/experiment.py:121:    marker = \"CA_EVENT \"\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src/consensus_assurance/adapters/runners/cargo.py:19:    harness_instructions = \"Write Rust #[test] integration tests using the captured crate's public API and existing dependencies. The controller fixes the workspace lock and tools; build reuse is specific to a manifest, integration test target and saved build basis. Switching crates may require a first heavy dependency build. Consult research.json build_inputs for preparation evidence and current seed availability; unlisted targets are not known to be prepared, and available seeds still require execution-time validation. Only the generated test target runs, serially with --nocapture and --locked --offline. Emit complete CA_EVENT JSON lines. Helpers are workspace-relative; never replace manifests, locks, build scripts or Cargo configuration. No extra feature flags are added; original dev-dependencies can enable additional features. Actual compiler-artifact records describe reuse; declaration syntax does not establish active cfg branches or private-state reachability.\"\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src/consensus_assurance/adapters/runners/python.py:9:    harness_instructions = \"Use Python imports from the current isolated workspace and print CA_EVENT JSON containing actual returned values.\"\n/home/nitro/Desktop/consensus-assurance/src/consensus_assurance/resources/skills/evidence-review/references/experiments.md:5:CORRELATION Monitor.admission_alias explicitly selects the declared harness prerequisite admitting operations whose completion is owed. It is not the first prerequisite by array order. Harness.prerequisites contains prerequisite events only; monitors select independent result events. ObservableProperty.identity_fields declares the stable operation identity once, using fields present at the relevant observation points. Do not repeat a result as a prerequisite or duplicate each identity equality as a Comparison. Reference conditions declare necessary predecessor relationships; unrelated prerequisites have no implicit list order. Assertions may refer to any declared prerequisite alias that actually precedes the result. Missing identity/reference fields or nonunique associations remain unknown. Independent applicability is checked before requiring unrelated history. Alias-dependent applicability follows correlation. Each operation is matched separately. A complete violating witness survives missing results in demonstrably independent operations, while coverage remains partial. Same-witness ambiguity and potentially global parse loss still block attribution. Use independent identity, not the compared value, to associate an input and output. A context expected to change cannot also be an equality filter. More complex histories need actual correlated prerequisites and results; unsupported general temporal expressions remain a stated limitation.\n    72\tpub enum SnapshotType<T>\n    73\twhere\n    74\t    T: Entry,\n    75\t{\n    76\t    Complete(T::Snapshot),\n    77\t    Delta(T::Snapshot),\n    78\t}\n    79\t\n    80\t/// Trait for implementing snapshot operations for log entries of type `T` in OmniPaxos.\n    81\tpub trait Snapshot<T>: Clone + Debug\n    82\twhere\n    83\t    T: Entry,\n    84\t{\n    85\t    /// Create a snapshot from the log `entries`.\n    86\t    fn create(entries: &[T]) -> Self;\n    87\t\n    88\t    /// Merge another snapshot `delta` into self.\n    89\t    fn merge(&mut self, delta: Self);\n    90\t\n    91\t    /// Whether `T` is snapshottable. If not, simply return `false` and leave the other functions `unimplemented!()`.\n    92\t    fn use_snapshots() -> bool;\n    93\t\n    94\t    //fn size_hint() -> usize;  // TODO: To let the system know trade-off of using entries vs snapshot?\n    95\t}\n    96\t\n    97\t/// The Result type returned by the storage API.\n    98\tpub type StorageResult<T> = Result<T, Box<dyn Error>>;\n    99\t\n   100\t/// The write operations of the storge implementation.\n   101\t#[derive(Debug)]\n   102\tpub enum StorageOp<T: Entry> {\n   103\t    /// Appends an entry to the end of the log.\n   104\t    AppendEntry(T),\n   105\t    /// Appends entries to the end of the log.\n    27\t> **Note:** Make sure your application really does not need the data that will be trimmed anymore. Once it is succeeded, the trimmed entries are lost and cannot be read or recovered.\n    28\t\n    29\t## Snapshot\n    30\tTrimming compacts the log and discards any data preceding the trim index. For safety, it therefore requires all servers to have decided the trim index. If you don't want to discard any data and the entries in the log are such that they can be compacted into a snapshot, `OmniPaxos` supports snapshotting decided entries of the log. For instance, in our kv-store example, we don't need to keep every log entry that changes the kv-pairs. Instead, if we want to snapshot the log, it is sufficient to keep the latest value for every key. We implement our snapshot as a struct called `KVSnapshot` which is just a wrapper for a `HashMap` that will hold the latest value for every key in the log. To make it work with `OmniPaxos`, we need to implement the trait `Snapshot` for `KVSnapshot`:\n    31\t\n    32\t```rust\n    33\tuse std::collections::HashMap;\n    34\tuse omnipaxos::storage::Snapshot;\n    35\t\n    36\t#[derive(Clone, Debug)]\n    37\tpub struct KVSnapshot {\n    38\t    snapshotted: HashMap<String, u64>\n    39\t}\n    40\t\n    41\timpl Snapshot<KeyValue> for KVSnapshot {\n    42\t    fn create(entries: &[KeyValue]) -> Self {\n    43\t        let mut snapshotted = HashMap::new();\n    44\t        for e in entries {\n    45\t            let KeyValue { key, value } = e;\n    46\t            snapshotted.insert(key.clone(), *value);\n    47\t        }\n    48\t        Self { snapshotted }\n    49\t    }\n    50\t\n    51\t    fn merge(&mut self, delta: Self) {\n    52\t        for (k, v) in delta.snapshotted {\n    53\t            self.snapshotted.insert(k, v);\n    54\t        }\n    55\t    }\n    56\t\n    57\t    fn use_snapshots() -> bool {\n    58\t        true\n    59\t    }\n    60\t}\n    61\t``` \n    62\t\n    63\tThe ``create()`` function tells `OmniPaxos` how to create a snapshot given a slice of entries of our `KeyValue` type. In our case, we simply want to insert the kv-pair into the hashmap. The `merge()` function defines how we can merge two snapshots. In our case, we will just insert/update the kv-pairs from the other snapshot. The `use_snapshots()` function tells `OmniPaxos` if snapshots should be used in the protocol. \n    64\t\n    65\tWith ``KVSnapshot``, we would have instead implemented our [`KeyValue`](../omnipaxos) that we defined earlier like this:\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
